Description: CVE-2026-84384 - bound brotli/zlib decompression output by security limits
 libheif's decompression routines for 'mime' metadata and 'unci' compressed
 image data (using brotli and zlib) failed to enforce effective output-size
 limits, creating a decompression-bomb / data-amplification vulnerability
 (CWE-409, GHSA-24wx-9w62-c96w). The brotli path had no size check at all, and
 the zlib path's intended 256MB cap only checked the small intermediate scratch
 buffer rather than the accumulated output, so it never tripped. Neither path
 integrated with libheif's memory-management safeguards (heif_security_limits),
 so configured limits were ineffective and a small crafted HEIF file (as small
 as 784 bytes) could trigger unbounded memory allocation and an unrecoverable
 crash.
 .
 This is a backport of upstream commit 21893e8f to the pre-refactor 1.19.8
 codebase. The target lacks the upstream MemoryHandle abstraction and the
 max_total_memory security limit, so the decompression output is instead bounded
 against the existing per-block limit (max_memory_block_size): decompress_zlib,
 decompress_deflate and decompress_brotli gain a heif_security_limits* parameter
 and reject output that grows past the configured block size, and the unci icef
 path bounds the total accumulated output across all (possibly overlapping)
 units. All call sites in file.cc and codecs/uncompressed/decoder_abstract.cc
 thread the context's limits through. A regression test
 (tests/decompression_bomb.cc), adapted from the upstream test to the older test
 harness and the max_memory_block_size limit, is added.
