+TEST_CASE("brotli mime metadata decompression bomb is bounded by security limits") {
+#if HAVE_BROTLI
+  // A brotli payload that expands to ~40 MB but is only a few hundred bytes on disk.
+  const size_t decompressed_size = 40u * 1024 * 1024;
+  std::vector<uint8_t> zeros(decompressed_size, 0);
+  std::vector<uint8_t> bomb = compress_brotli(zeros.data(), zeros.size());
+  REQUIRE(!bomb.empty());
+  REQUIRE(bomb.size() < decompressed_size); // it really is highly compressible
+
+  std::vector<uint8_t> file = build_heif_with_brotli_mime(bomb);
+
+  // --- with a tight per-block memory limit, opening must fail rather than
+  //     allocate the whole decompressed payload (previously it ignored the
+  //     limit entirely).
+  {
+    heif_context* ctx = heif_context_alloc();
+    REQUIRE(ctx != nullptr);
+
+    heif_security_limits* limits = heif_context_get_security_limits(ctx);
+    REQUIRE(limits != nullptr);
+    limits->max_memory_block_size = 8u * 1024 * 1024; // 8 MB << 40 MB bomb
+
+    heif_error err = heif_context_read_from_memory_without_copy(ctx, file.data(), file.size(), nullptr);
+    REQUIRE(err.code != heif_error_Ok);
+    REQUIRE(err.subcode == heif_suberror_Security_limit_exceeded);
+
+    heif_context_free(ctx);
+  }
+
+  // --- with the default (generous) limits, the very same file opens successfully,
+  //     proving it is structurally valid and that the limit is what is enforced.
+  {
+    heif_context* ctx = heif_context_alloc();
+    REQUIRE(ctx != nullptr);
+
+    heif_error err = heif_context_read_from_memory_without_copy(ctx, file.data(), file.size(), nullptr);
+    REQUIRE(err.code == heif_error_Ok);
+
+    heif_context_free(ctx);
+  }
+#else
+  SUCCEED("brotli support not compiled in - skipping decompression bomb test");
+#endif
+}
Description: CVE-2026-84447 - Bound derived-image reference amplification (GHSA-x8xm-cm2c-cfc8)
 Derived images (grid/iovl/iden) can reference a shared base image through
 indirection. The per-path cycle-detection set is copied by value, so it forks
 at every recursion branch and never acts as a visited-memo. A shared subtree is
 therefore re-decoded once per path that reaches it, and nested references make
 the number of decode operations grow as branch^depth. A tiny file can thus
 request an effectively unbounded amount of decoding work (CPU DoS), and the
 file-open cycle check had the same defect, hanging before any decode.
 .
 This is a hand-adapted backport of upstream commit 30b52a4e to libheif 1.19.8,
 whose decode API predates the upstream DecodeTraversalState construct. The same
 defenses are reimplemented against the older, parameter-less decode_image API:
 .
  * libheif/file.cc: HeifFile::check_for_ref_cycle_recursion now memoizes
    fully-verified (acyclic) items in a finished_items set, turning the
    exponential per-path DFS into a linear white/gray/black DFS. This stops the
    file-open hang on shared/nested derived-image references.
  * libheif/context.{h,cc}: HeifContext now owns a shared atomic decode budget,
    seeded once per top-level HeifContext::decode_image() at a small multiple of
    max_items (MAX_DERIVED_IMAGE_DECODE_FACTOR). Because it is atomic it also
    bounds the parallel tile-decode threads. A budget of 0 (limits disabled)
    means unlimited.
  * libheif/image-items/image_item.cc: ImageItem::decode_image(), the single
    choke point every item decode passes through, counts each sub-image decode
    against the shared budget and rejects with heif_suberror_Security_limit_exceeded
    once it is exhausted. This is the structure-agnostic backstop against the
    exponential blow-up.
  * libheif/image-items/overlay.cc: read_overlay_spec() rejects an 'iovl' that
    composites more than MAX_OVERLAY_IMAGES input images (hardcoded stopgap).
  * libheif/security_limits.h: adds MAX_OVERLAY_IMAGES and
    MAX_DERIVED_IMAGE_DECODE_FACTOR.
 .
 Only internal C++ signatures change; the public C API and ABI are unaffected.
 A regression test (tests/overlay_amplification.cc, from the upstream commit) is
 included: a deeply nested overlay gadget and an over-wide overlay are now
 rejected in milliseconds instead of hanging, while a shallow legitimate overlay
 still decodes.
